      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's delve into the nitty-gritty of the N-P-K breakdown and the chemical impact on soil pH levels for both blood meal and bone meal.\n\nBlood meal is an organic fertilizer that is approximately 12% nitrogen (N), 0% phosphorus (P), and 0% potassium (K). It is a concentrated source of nitrogen, which can provide a quick boost to your soil's nitrogen levels. However, because it is so rich in nitrogen, it can potentially lower the soil pH over time, especially if applied in large quantities. This is due to the ammonium (NH4+) in blood meal, which can acidify the soil as it is converted to nitrate (NO3-) by soil bacteria.\n\nBone meal, on the other hand, is an organic fertilizer that is approximately 15% phosphorus (P), 0% nitrogen (N), and 0% potassium (K). It is a slower-releasing source of phosphorus, which can help improve root development and flowering in plants. Bone meal is less likely to affect soil pH because it does not contain a high concentration of nitrogen. However, it is important to note that bone meal can also slightly lower the soil pH over",
